WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
Storage, initialization, and validity are related, but they are not identical.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:09.000
Storage means there is memory for an object representation.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.580
Initialization means the program has placed that storage into a known state.

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:21.040
Validity means the object can be used for a particular operation according to its type, invariants, ownership, and lifetime rules.

5
00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.440
Uninitialized storage should not drive decisions.

6
00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:30.300
A dynamic allocation may succeed, but its fields may not yet represent a valid object.

7
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.720
A local structure may have storage, but not all members may have been assigned.

8
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:41.140
A buffer may have capacity, but it may not contain initialized bytes or a terminated string.

9
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.620
Partial construction needs tracked progress.

10
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:52.960
A constructor-style function may allocate a structure, initialize one member, open a resource, and then fail while initializing another member.

11
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:57.780
The failure path should release only what was successfully initialized and owned.

12
00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.840
That requires the construction state to be visible.

13
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.720
Ownership transfer is also a state transition.

14
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:12.820
Once an object is handed to a container or callee that takes ownership, the previous owner should no longer clean it up or assume it can freely mutate it.

15
00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:18.040
If transfer happens only on success, the API should define what happens on failure.

16
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.800
Destroyed objects must not be reused.

17
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:25.300
A cleanup function can end the lifetime of an object or of resources inside it.

18
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:31.320
After that point, pointer values may still exist, but they no longer prove valid access.

19
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:35.260
Code should not inspect the old object as if it were still alive.

20
00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:39.220
Explicit state transitions make lifetime behavior reviewable.

21
00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:49.080
An object moves from absent, to allocated, to initialized, to valid for use, possibly to transferred, and eventually to destroyed or invalid.

22
00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:53.580
Defensive design makes those transitions clear in code and documentation.
